{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And we cried out to HaShem, the Elohim of our fathers.</b> For were it not for the merit of the fathers, He would not have heard our prayer, because they were in Egypt wicked and sinful, as is found in the prophecy of Ezekiel (chapter 20). This is what it says: ״וַיַּרְא אֶת עָנְיֵנוּ וְאֶת עֲמָלֵנוּ וְאֶת לַחֲצֵנוּ״. And you shall know that 'עָנִי' and 'לַחַץ' are written in parashat Shemot (Exodus 3:7–9): ״רָאֹה רָאִיתִי אֶת עֳנִי עַמִּי״ and so on, and ״וְגַם רָאִיתִי אֶת הַלַּחַץ״ and so on; but the seeing of our 'עָמָל' we do not find written. Therefore it appears that ״עֲמָלֵנוּ״ means that He looked upon toil and evil in Jacob and in the children of Israel — that even at the time when they were in affliction and oppression from every side, a man did not cast away the detestable things of his eyes. And the Holy One, blessed be He, saw the bond and its cancellation — the affliction and the toil that are ours. And if so, surely were it not for the merit of our fathers He would not have answered us. ״וַיּוֹצִיאֵנוּ ה׳ מִמִּצְרַיִם בְּיָד חֲזָקָה״ — against the attribute of judgment that indicted us, saying: how do these differ from those — these are idol worshippers and those are idol worshippers, etc. And from all of this is a proof for ״וַיְבִיאֵנוּ אֶל הַמָּקוֹם הַזֶּה וַיִּתֶּן לָנוּ״ — as a gift — ״הָאָרֶץ״. ״וְעַתָּה״ — what has passed has passed, and from here forward is an accounting, for now I acknowledge that to HaShem belongs the land, and the miracle proving this is ״הִנֵּה הֵבֵאתִי״ and so on. ״וְהִנַּחְתּוֹ לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — for from the time he drew near to HaShem." "And Mahar'i explained: ״וְהִנַּחְתּוֹ״ — that he should leave it there, from the language of ״וַתַּנַַח בִּגְדוֹ אֶצְלָהּ״ (Genesis 39:16), that he should not go back and take the basket from there, but rather leave it for the priests of HaShem."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The citation 'בנבואת יחזקאל (כח)' likely refers to Ezekiel chapter 20, which describes Israel's idolatry in Egypt; the chapter number is given as 28 in the source but the content matches chapter 20. The phrase 'איש שקוצי עיניו לא השליכו' alludes to Ezekiel 20:7–8. 'שטר ושוברו' is a legal idiom: the bond and its tear-up, i.e., God saw both the debt and its cancellation.", 1 "מהרי״א (Mahar'i) is apparently R. Joseph Bekhor Shor or another commentator known by this acronym; resolved as Mahar'i without further identification."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:20.720165774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}